.TH smi-serve-files "1" "August 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-serve-files \- Serve an application directory over HTTP behind a reverse proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-serve-files
.I COUNTRY_CODE ORG_SHORT_NAME APP_NAME
.RI [ PORT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Serves
.IR $(smi-applications-location\ CC\ ORG)/APP_NAME/www
over HTTP with an Elixir server, for
.BR haproxy (1)
or
.BR nginx (8)
to sit in front of.
Binds to
.B 127.0.0.1
only.
Runs in the foreground until killed.
.PP
An application is two sibling directories:
.PP
.RS
.I APP_NAME/www
\- what is served. Root owned, read only to everybody else.
.br
.I APP_NAME/workingdir
\- what the service writes. Owned by
.BR microservice ,
mode
.BR 700 .
.RE
.PP
Only
.I www
is served, so nothing the service user can write is reachable over HTTP.
.PP
Must be started as root.
It sets
.I www
to
.B root:root
with mode
.B 644
for files and
.B 755
for directories, creates
.I workingdir
owned by
.B microservice
with mode
.BR 700 ,
and then drops to the
.B microservice
user for the server itself.
The service user can therefore read the site but not modify it.
.PP
.I workingdir
is the service user's
.B HOME
and the only place it may write: log files, runtime state, and the
.B Mix.install
dependency cache, so the first run needs network access to fetch the dependencies.
.PP
A request that matches no file is answered with
.IR index.html ,
so a single page application resolves its own routes.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I COUNTRY_CODE
ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code of the organization.
.TP
.I ORG_SHORT_NAME
Organization short name.
.TP
.I APP_NAME
Directory name under the organization's applications directory.
.PP
None of the three may contain a
.B /
or start with a
.BR . :
each becomes a path component and this command runs
.B chown -R
as root.
.TP
.I PORT
Port to listen on.
Defaults to
.BR 8130 .
.SH EXAMPLE PAGE
.PP
The toolset ships one static page,
.BR hello-server ,
to check that serving works before pointing this at a real application.
It is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no backend calls: it renders a greeting, and its
script replaces one line of text, so a correct render means the HTML, the stylesheet, the script
and an
.I assets/
file were all served.
.PP
It is installed with the toolset rather than under an organization, because the applications
directory is per organization and is only known at deploy time.
Copy it in as the application's
.IR www :
.PP
.RS
.B APPS=$(smi-applications-location ee has)
.br
.B sudo mkdir -p ${APPS}/hello-server
.br
.B sudo cp -R $(smi-lib-location)/hello-server/. ${APPS}/hello-server/www
.br
.B sudo env PATH="$PATH" smi-serve-files ee has hello-server
.RE
.PP
The
.B /.
at the end of the source is required, and is the whole difference between
.I www/index.html
and
.IR www/hello-server/index.html .
Without it
.B cp -R
copies the directory
.I into
.I www
whenever
.I www
already exists.
With it the
.I contents
are copied, whether
.I www
exists or not, so the command can be repeated to update the site.
.I workingdir
needs no
.BR mkdir :
this command creates it.
.PP
.B env PATH="$PATH"
is what lets root find the toolset at all.
.I /opt/setmy.info/bin
is put on
.B PATH
by
.IR /etc/profile.d/setmy-info.sh ,
which root under
.B sudo
has not read.
On a distribution whose sudoers sets
.BR secure_path ,
Red Hat family ones do by default,
.B sudo
uses that path to look the command up and plain
.B sudo smi-serve-files
fails with
.BR "command not found" .
.PP
.B sudo --preserve-env=PATH
does not fix it either:
.B secure_path
still decides how the command is found, and the flag only affects the environment handed to a
command already resolved.
.B env
works because it is itself on
.BR secure_path ,
so
.B sudo
finds
.BR env ,
and
.B env
then resolves
.B smi-serve-files
using the
.B PATH
it was given.
That
.B PATH
also stays in place for the
.B smi-*
commands this one calls.
.PP
The alternative, if you would rather not repeat this on every invocation, is to add
.I /opt/setmy.info/bin
to
.B secure_path
in
.IR /etc/sudoers .
.PP
.B $(smi-lib-location)
and
.B $(smi-applications-location)
in the lines above are different: they are expanded by the calling shell before
.B sudo
starts, so they use the caller's own
.BR PATH .
A root shell started without that profile expands them to nothing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $(smi-applications-location\ CC\ ORG)/APP_NAME/www
The served directory.
Root owned, read only to everybody else.
.TP
.I $(smi-applications-location\ CC\ ORG)/APP_NAME/workingdir
Service user's home, log files, runtime state and dependency cache.
Never served.
.TP
.I $(smi-lib-location)/hello-server
The example page, before it is copied into an organization.
.TP
.I $(smi-lib-location)/elixir/smi-serve-files.exs
The server.
.TP
.IR /opt/erlang ,\  /opt/elixir
The runtime.
The
.B PATH
given to the service user is built from these two, so the server always runs on the toolset's
own Erlang and Elixir rather than on whatever the caller or the distribution provides.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 1
Bad arguments, not run as root, missing directory, missing
.B microservice
user, or elixir not found.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.B sudo env PATH="$PATH" smi-serve-files ee has hello-server
.br
.B sudo env PATH="$PATH" smi-serve-files ee has my-app
.br
.B sudo env PATH="$PATH" smi-serve-files ee has my-app 8081
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR smi-applications-location (1),
.BR smi-lib-location (1),
.BR elixir (1)
.SH AUTHOR
setmy.info
